\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {36.1}{\ignorespaces The eight Amendment\nobreakspace {}8 hypotheses. Blue arms reuse saved Study\nobreakspace {}2 artifacts (no retraining); gold arms train new models on seeds no earlier arm used; F8 pools the three dose-response arms.\relax }}{107}{figure.caption.81}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {37.1}{\ignorespaces How F1 defines a discordant cell. A slide-level label prior would pull discordant cells toward their slide's majority class when the slide was seen in training; the models did the opposite.\relax }}{108}{figure.caption.82}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {37.2}{\ignorespaces Rank-limited LEACE erasure (F5). At full rank the projection is LEACE for the within-class parent indicator: no linear function of the erased features covaries with it on the fitting data. Ranks 0, 4, 16, 64, and 256 give the dose.\relax }}{109}{figure.caption.83}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {37.3}{\ignorespaces Within-class recoverability from the registered sklearn probe and the device probe used for F5--F8, on the 280 Study\nobreakspace {}2 adversarial runs.\relax }}{110}{figure.caption.84}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {39.1}{\ignorespaces Left: the leak advantage under each test-time ablation (F2 highlighted). Middle: the leak effect under each training condition on fresh seeds (F3, F4). Right: CRIC within-cell accuracy gains by concordance, and the context-class probability for discordant cells (F1).\relax }}{113}{figure.caption.85}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {39.2}{\ignorespaces The carrier factorial per architecture.\relax }}{113}{figure.caption.86}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {39.3}{\ignorespaces Accuracy of leak and clean 2C models under each test-time ablation, per architecture.\relax }}{114}{figure.caption.87}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {39.4}{\ignorespaces Exploratory: the leak advantage on test crops that do or do not share source pixels with a training sibling.\relax }}{115}{figure.caption.88}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {39.5}{\ignorespaces F1 per architecture, and the within-cell accuracy gain for concordant and discordant cells.\relax }}{116}{figure.caption.89}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {39.6}{\ignorespaces The P1 $-$ P2 gap against recoverability along three manipulations (grey: architectures; colour: pooled over architectures that passed the manipulation check, at levels they share; registered in-regime levels only).\relax }}{116}{figure.caption.90}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {39.7}{\ignorespaces LEACE per architecture (grey) and pooled (blue): recoverability, gap, and P2 accuracy change by erasure rank.\relax }}{117}{figure.caption.91}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {39.8}{\ignorespaces The full linear-adversary sweep per architecture, including the degenerate strengths ($\lambda =1$ for ResNet-50 and DenseNet-121, $\lambda =3$ for all) where P2 accuracy collapses and which the regime rule excludes.\relax }}{117}{figure.caption.92}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {39.9}{\ignorespaces The stain-normalisation sweep per architecture: accuracy falls for both protocols, recoverability falls only modestly.\relax }}{118}{figure.caption.93}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {39.10}{\ignorespaces Left: per-run Grad-CAM attribution outside the cell for leak and clean models (bar: background pixel share). Right: agreement of each run's maps with random-head maps.\relax }}{121}{figure.caption.94}%
